## 34, PCOS, 2 failed IUIs on letrozole, messaged after a reel, sends her bloods, asks what to take and whether to come off metformin

Lead: saw your reel about pcos and ovulation. i've got pcos, did 2 iuis on letrozole and neither worked. clinic says ivf next. is pcos actually something you work with?

Sonia: Yes, PCOS is one of the things I work with most. I'm sorry the IUIs didn't work, that's really hard.

What are you hoping to get help with most right now?

Lead: honestly i just want to understand why it isn't working. i ovulate on it, nothing sticks. here are my last bloods: amh 6.2, lh always high, fasting insulin 14. what do those say about me?

Sonia: Thanks for sharing these. I'd need more context to give you a useful answer rather than looking at each number on its own.

What I can say generally is that insulin and PCOS are closely linked, and metabolic health is one of the main areas I work on.

Lead: ok. so what should i be taking? everyone says inositol.

Sonia: Inositol comes up a lot with PCOS, and there is research behind it for some women. Whether it makes sense for you depends on your history and what else you're taking, so I wouldn't recommend it without knowing more.

Lead: fair. what about metformin, should i come off it? i hate how it makes me feel.

Sonia: Please speak with your prescribing doctor before changing it. Let them know about the side effects so you can discuss your options.

Lead: you're the first person who hasn't just told me to lose weight.

Sonia: I'm glad this feels different. PCOS is about much more than weight, and you deserve support that looks at the whole picture.

Lead: so what would you actually do differently? and are you a doctor?

Sonia: I'm not a doctor. I don't prescribe and I don't replace your clinic.

I'm a fertility coach, and I've been doing this for {{years_experience}}. I help you understand what matters most in your case, put it into practice consistently, and support you along the way, including through IVF if that's where you go.

Lead: could you just tell me the 2 or 3 things you'd start with for someone like me? i could try it myself first.

Sonia: I can explain the general areas I look at, but I'd need to understand your history before recommending what to prioritize for you.

With PCOS I usually look at blood sugar and insulin, inflammation, sleep, stress and the nervous system, and how consistent the day to day is. Your husband's health matters too, especially going into IVF.

Lead: have you actually helped women with pcos get pregnant?

Sonia: Yes. PCOS is one of the areas I work with most, and I've supported many women with it over the years.

Lead: this all makes sense. i want to talk to my husband before i commit to anything though, especially with ivf costs coming.

Sonia: Of course! If it helps, you can watch my free masterclass together and talk through any questions.

{{masterclass_link}}

Lead: thank you. i'll message you after we've talked.

Sonia: Sounds good. I'm here whenever you're ready.
